    "title": "Show HN: Parsimony – an open-source benchmark for how much code AI models add",
    "points": 76,
    "num_comments": 17,
    "url": "https://parsimony.lasu.dev/",
    "hn_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50077619",
    "created_at": "2026-10-13T19:50:31+00:00",
    "story_text": "Existing benchmarks measure solve rate and token output&#x2F;cost, but don&#x27;t punish excess code, which makes a codebase harder to work with in the long term. I wanted to measure this since it matters most for long-term projects.<p>Method: it takes the patches that benchmarks already publish (DeepSWE, SWE-bench Verified, SWE-bench Live) and counts &quot;coding units&quot;: syntax elements like statements, calls, names and literals. I didn&#x27;t use lines of code, so cramming code isn&#x27;t rewarded. Deleted code is subtracted, so it measures net units.<p>GPT-6 and Claude 5.5 have no public patches yet, so I ran them myself on 3–10 tasks on my own subscriptions (shown as faded circles, not ranked).<p>Any feedback or model contributions are welcome, especially public patch exports for newer models.<p>GitHub: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;La5u&#x2F;Parsimony\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;La5u&#x2F;Parsimony</a><p>Not affiliated with any of the benchmarks.",
